#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725 :topic/id #uuid "b8bf3ca3-98f8-5ec7-8c13-6f1988c58838" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054781 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054411 :question/id #uuid "ca811d3a-a7be-536b-b997-dd2355cb00d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12999" :question/problem "Find the solution to the equation $16 + x =10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the left-hand-side so that it's similar to what we've seen before.\n\n$$\nx + 16 = 10\n$$\n\nTo isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding $16$ to $x$, which is *subtracting* $16$ from $x.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \t\tx + 16 &= 10 \\\\\n    \t\tx + 16 - 16 &= 10 - 16 \\\\\n\t\tx + 0 &= -6 \\\\\n\t\tx &= -6\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c05c87-f04c-5f15-96f6-45287dd45876" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations that Require Subtraction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054782 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054412 :question/id #uuid "094ae644-0ce0-51e7-849f-38320770df93" :question/math-academy-id "e-13000" :question/problem "Solve the equation $x-8 =6.$" :question/worked-solution "To isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $8$ from $x$, which is *adding* $8$ to $x.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \t\tx-8 &= 6 \\\\\n\t\tx-8+8 &= 6+8 \\\\\n\t\tx+0 &= 14 \\\\\n\t\tx &= 14\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3163647c-e7ba-545e-b4a7-a8a3fff2125f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations that Require Addition"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054783 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054413 :question/id #uuid "2d414c47-6dff-54f2-a23a-ee071f8f43fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13001" :question/problem "What is the solution to the equation $-15 = p - 12?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\np-12 = -15\n$$\n\nTo isolate $p$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $12$ from $p$, which is *adding* $12$ to $p.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np-12 \t\t&= -15\\\\\np-12 + 12 \t&= -15 + 12\\\\\np+0\t\t\t&=-3\\\\\np\t\t\t&=-3    \t\t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51a61370-93c1-503c-bb53-af6442794f20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations Containing Negative Constants"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054784 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054414 :question/id #uuid "20d2de55-4713-570b-a5df-dc1a52b6fbc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13002" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{4} + z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{4}+z \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+z & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo isolate $z$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ to $z$, which is *subtracting* $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ from $z.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ z+0 & =\\frac{2}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2-3}{4} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82b22101-e915-51b6-a5f3-78a1f286c2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations Containing Non-Integer Constants"}] :topic/math-academy-id 65 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049986}] :topic/title "Solving One-Step Addition and Subtraction Equations"}]}
